<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikola Karev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikola Karev was a Macedonian revolutionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization">Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> of August 1903 he led the rebels who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a> and headed its ten-day provisional government, the Kruševo Republic, before Ottoman troops retook the town. He was killed in an ambush in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikola-karev.jpg" alt="Nikola Karev" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: published anonymously · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikola_Karev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikola Yanakiev Karev was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a> on 23 November 1877 and went to the Bulgarian Exarchist school there. In 1893 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, where he worked as a carpenter for the socialist Vasil Glavinov, met Dimitar Blagoev and joined the Bulgarian Workers' Social Democratic Party. Returning to Ottoman Macedonia in 1898, he graduated from the Exarchist gymnasium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and from 1900 taught in Exarchist schools in Gorno Divjaci and Kruševo. That year a conference of Macedonian socialists near Kruševo called for a multinational Macedonian republic within a Balkan socialist federation, and Karev joined the revolutionary organisation as leader of a regional band, or cheta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In May 1903 he told Stamatis Stamatiou of the Greek daily Akropolis that the revolutionaries wanted a republic on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, giving autonomy and democracy to its different peoples. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a> fell to the rebels in August, he became president of the provisional government of the Kruševo Republic. He is said to have written the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo-manifesto">Kruševo Manifesto</a>, which called on local Muslims to join the Christians. A council of 60 was elected, 20 each from the Bulgarian Exarchists, Aromanians and Greek Patriarchists, with a government of six. The insurgents killed several Patriarchists accused of spying and attacked local Muslims, while Karev tried to limit the attacks and the looting. On 12 August he withdrew into the hills.</p><p>He went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and joined the Narrow Socialists, and his requests for an Ottoman amnesty went unanswered. On 16 March 1905 his cheta re-entered Ottoman territory, and soon after he was killed in fighting with Ottoman soldiers near the village of Rajčani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, historians of Socialist Macedonia made the Kruševo Republic a forerunner of the new republic, with Karev as its socialist link. His remains were moved to Kruševo in 1953, kept in the museum's storage, and in April 1990 placed in the Ilinden memorial. He is considered a national hero in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and an equestrian statue of him stands before the Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikola Karev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rebels under <b>Nikola Karev</b> and Pitu Guli seized Kruševo, a prosperous mountain town of Slav, Aromanian and Greek-oriented Orthodox families, and proclaimed a provisional government.</blockquote><a href="/state/krusevo-republic">Kruševo Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Died in Rajčani, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.37,21.25&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Kruševo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5069 689 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikola Karev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5519 959h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5519 959h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.37,21.25&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
